(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-respiratory-infections-vignettes"]={"title":"Respiratory Infections and Vaccines Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 35-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia confirmed on chest radiograph is stable for outpatient treatment. He has no comorbidities, no severe features and no resistant-organism risk. Which test is low value and should be skipped?","choices":["Pulse oximetry","Vital signs and sepsis screen","Severity index scoring","Routine sputum culture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cultures are obtained selectively, for severe community-acquired pneumonia, resistant-pathogen risk or treatment failure, and routine cultures do not help every stable outpatient. He has none of those triggers, so a culture would not change any decision. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 125"},{"q":"A 20-year-old college student has had 10 days of gradual fever, a persistent cough and headache. Several students in her dormitory have the same illness, and a respiratory nucleic acid test is positive for Mycoplasma pneumoniae. She has taken amoxicillin for 3 days without improvement. Which change is most appropriate?","choices":["Add clavulanate","Switch to cefpodoxime","Extend the amoxicillin course","Switch to azithromycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta-lactams are ineffective against Mycoplasma because it lacks a cell wall, so amoxicillin cannot work. Adults with Mycoplasma infection are treated with a macrolide such as azithromycin or with doxycycline, and a respiratory fluoroquinolone is an alternative. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman who is 30 weeks pregnant received Tdap during her last pregnancy 2 years ago. Which plan for pertussis protection is appropriate?","choices":["Wait 10 years from her last Tdap","Td instead of Tdap","Tdap after delivery","Tdap during this pregnancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tdap is given during every pregnancy, regardless of the interval since the last dose. Her dose 2 years ago in an earlier pregnancy does not cover this one, so she receives Tdap now. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with community-acquired pneumonia is alert and oriented. Her blood urea nitrogen is 26 mg/dL, respiratory rate 24/min and blood pressure 128/76 mm Hg. What is her CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score?","choices":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Confusion 0 (alert and oriented) + urea 1 (26 mg/dL is above 20) + respiratory rate 0 (24 is below 30) + blood pressure 0 (systolic not below 90, diastolic not 60 or lower) + age 1 (72) = 2. A total of 2 is moderate severity, with a risk of death of 9%, and the pathway sends her to the hospital. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 31"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia remains hypotensive after intravenous fluids and needs a vasopressor infusion to keep up his blood pressure. He is not intubated. How should the severity of his pneumonia be classified?","choices":["Nonsevere: he is not ventilated","Nonsevere: minor criteria not counted","Moderate: hospital ward care","Severe: vasopressor-dependent shock"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe community-acquired pneumonia is vasopressor-dependent shock, mechanical ventilation, or at least three minor criteria. Needing a vasopressor to hold his blood pressure is vasopressor-dependent shock, a major criterion on its own, so he needs urgent intensive care-level assessment and a beta-lactam combination regimen. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with human immunodeficiency virus infection is admitted with moderate Pneumocystis pneumonia confirmed on induced sputum. He has no drug allergies and normal kidney function. Which treatment is preferred?","choices":["Intravenous pentamidine","Atovaquone","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Clindamycin plus primaquine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is the preferred treatment for Pneumocystis pneumonia, given intravenously for moderate-to-severe disease and switched to oral therapy after improvement, for 21 days in disease linked to human immunodeficiency virus. Nothing in his history, no allergy and no kidney problem, moves him to an alternative. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man hospitalized for 6 days after a stroke develops hospital-acquired pneumonia. He has no methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus risk, no resistance risk and no high mortality risk. Which empiric agent is most appropriate?","choices":["Piperacillin-tazobactam","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Moxifloxacin","Vancomycin as a single drug"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hospital-acquired pneumonia without major resistance or high mortality risk is treated with an antipseudomonal agent that also covers methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus, chosen with the local antibiogram, such as piperacillin-tazobactam or cefepime. Respiratory cultures are obtained first. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 39, 42, 46"},{"q":"A 70-year-old unvaccinated man with diabetes and heart failure has mild coronavirus disease 2019 that began 6 days ago. His oxygen saturation is 96% on room air. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir","Molnupiravir","Dexamethasone","A 3-day remdesivir course"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A high-risk outpatient with mild or moderate disease and no hypoxemia is offered nirmatrelvir/ritonavir within 5 days or a three-day intravenous remdesivir course within 7 days of symptom onset. At day 6 only the remdesivir window is still open, and his age, comorbidities and lack of vaccination make him high risk. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 113, 120, 122"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia has a PSI (Pneumonia Severity Index) total of 104 points. Which risk class does that total fall in?","choices":["Class II","Class III","Class IV","Class V"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. On the interpretation table, 91 to 130 points is Class IV, with a mortality of 9.3%. The index plus clinical judgment is the preferred support for the admission decision, and a total just over 100 already carries roughly a 1 in 10 risk of death. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 30, 31"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man is admitted to a general ward with nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia and no resistant-organism risk. Macrolides and fluoroquinolones are unsuitable for him because of severe reactions to each class. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Ceftriaxone plus doxycycline","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Moxifloxacin monotherapy","Ceftriaxone plus vancomycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonsevere inpatient pneumonia is usually treated with a beta-lactam plus a macrolide, or with respiratory fluoroquinolone monotherapy. A beta-lactam plus doxycycline is the alternative when macrolides and fluoroquinolones are unsuitable, as his reactions make them. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with human immunodeficiency virus infection has never had Pneumocystis pneumonia. His CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) count is 160 cells/mm³, and his viral load is detectable. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start primary prophylaxis","Wait until the count is below 100","Prophylaxis after a first episode","No prophylaxis while he feels well"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Primary prophylaxis starts for a CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) count below 100 cells/mm³, or a count of 100 to 200 with a detectable viral load. His 160 with a detectable viral load meets the second criterion, and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is the preferred drug. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman with dysphagia after a stroke is recovering from aspiration pneumonia on a general ward. Which measure best addresses her risk of another episode?","choices":["Routine chest physiotherapy","Lying flat for meals","A swallowing assessment","Long-term prophylactic antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After aspiration pneumonia, assess swallowing, oral hygiene, positioning and reversible risks, and address recurrent aspiration risk. Dysphagia is a listed risk factor, so a swallowing assessment targets the cause of her aspiration. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 49, 50, 51"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man admitted with nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia is improving on ceftriaxone plus azithromycin. On day 3 his blood cultures grow Streptococcus pneumoniae that is susceptible to narrower agents. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue both for 14 days","Narrow to the culture results","Add vancomycin","Switch to cefepime"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are reassessed daily and narrowed when possible, and therapy is narrowed to culture and susceptibility results. He is improving and his organism and its susceptibilities are known, so broad empiric coverage is no longer needed; duration follows clinical stability. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 40, 45"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man tore down an old barn where bats roosted. Six days later he has high fever, chills, myalgias and a nonproductive cough. An early urine Histoplasma antigen is negative, but suspicion remains high. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Histoplasmosis is excluded","Amphotericin-based induction","Serum cryptococcal antigen","Repeat antigen testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Early negative tests do not exclude histoplasmosis. When suspicion persists, testing is repeated or respiratory specimens are obtained. His bat exposure and typical illness keep suspicion high, so one early negative result does not end the workup. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man gets hives whenever he eats eggs and asks whether he can receive this season's influenza vaccine. He has never reacted to an influenza vaccine. What is the most appropriate plan?","choices":["Vaccinate under specialist supervision","Routine vaccination and safety steps","An egg-free product","Skip influenza vaccination"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Egg allergy does not require an egg-free product or precautions beyond routine vaccine safety measures. He has never reacted to an influenza vaccine, so he receives an age- and health-appropriate vaccine this season like any other adult. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 6"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who is 24 weeks pregnant has had 3 days of fever, myalgias and cough, and she is not short of breath. Influenza is strongly suspected, and a molecular test has been sent. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["A single dose of baloxavir","No antiviral after 48 hours","Await molecular confirmation","Start oseltamivir now"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pregnancy in any trimester is a high-risk group, and oseltamivir is preferred for high-risk patients, including in pregnancy. It is started promptly, even after 48 hours of symptoms, without waiting for confirmation in a high-risk patient. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 98, 108, 95, 105"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man who aspirated gastric contents during a witnessed seizure was managed with oxygen and airway support and no antibiotic. Three days later he is worse, with a new fever, purulent sputum and a persistent infiltrate. Which complication should be suspected?","choices":["Secondary bacterial infection","Resolving chemical injury","Viral acute bronchitis","Asthma exacerbation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pneumonitis is managed with support, and persistence or worsening raises concern for secondary infection, a complication or another diagnosis. New fever, purulent sputum and a persistent infiltrate after 3 days point to a secondary bacterial infection, which now needs the applicable pneumonia regimen. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman is admitted to a general ward with nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia. She has never had methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas isolated and has had no recent intravenous antibiotics. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Piperacillin-tazobactam","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Vancomycin plus cefepime","Amoxicillin as a single drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nonsevere inpatient pneumonia without resistant-organism risk is treated with a beta-lactam such as ceftriaxone or ampicillin-sulbactam plus a macrolide, or with respiratory fluoroquinolone monotherapy. She has no resistant-organism history, so no broader coverage is added. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has had 9 days of coughing fits that end in vomiting, with a whooping sound on inspiration. Her nephew was diagnosed with pertussis last week. Her vital signs and lung examination are normal. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Targeted pertussis testing","Reassess after 4 weeks of cough","Supportive care and reassurance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Paroxysmal cough, post-tussive vomiting, a whoop and a relevant exposure are the pertussis clues, and suspected pertussis needs targeted testing and treatment to reduce transmission. There is no need to wait for 4 weeks of cough before testing. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 17, 18"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman from a nursing home is admitted with fever, cough and shortness of breath while both influenza and coronavirus disease 2019 are circulating in the community. Which test should be sent?","choices":["Routine sputum culture","Multiplex molecular test","Rapid influenza antigen test","Viral culture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalized patients are tested with a molecular assay, and a multiplex influenza and coronavirus test is useful during co-circulation. Her age, nursing-home residence and admission make the result important for antivirals and infection prevention. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman being treated for community-acquired pneumonia becomes newly confused. Her heart rate is 118/min, blood pressure 84/50 mm Hg and oxygen saturation 87% on room air, and her urine output has fallen. Which complication has developed?","choices":["Empyema","Pleurisy","Lung abscess","Septicemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When pneumonia becomes systemic it progresses toward bacteremia or septicemia: tachycardia with hypotension, hypoxia, confusion or altered mental status, and decreased urine output or kidney function. She has every one of those signs, which changes her site of care and urgency of support. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 28, 53"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman without asthma or chronic lung disease has 8 days of cough after a cold, with a mild expiratory wheeze. She is afebrile with a normal oxygen saturation and no focal findings. Which treatment should not be prescribed routinely?","choices":["Acetaminophen as needed","Counseling on cough duration","Oral corticosteroids","A short bronchodilator trial"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Systemic or inhaled corticosteroids are not routinely prescribed for uncomplicated bronchitis. Her wheeze is the transient wheeze of bronchitis from inflammation and mucus, with no asthma history and no focal findings, so a steroid adds risk without benefit. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 17, 19, 20"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman is intubated in the intensive care unit for severe community-acquired pneumonia, and molecular testing confirms that it is influenza pneumonia. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":["Systemic corticosteroids","Oseltamivir","Empiric antibacterial therapy","Ventilatory support"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Systemic corticosteroids may be considered for severe community-acquired pneumonia, but influenza pneumonia is the stated exclusion. Her severe pneumonia is influenza pneumonia, so the steroid is the treatment to avoid while antiviral, antibacterial and respiratory support continue. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 47, 108"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman is expected to need mechanical ventilation for several days after a large stroke. Which measure helps prevent ventilator-associated pneumonia?","choices":["Prophylactic broad antibiotics","Elevating the head of the bed","Daily ventilator circuit changes","Keeping her lying flat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prevention is the main strategy for ventilator-associated pneumonia: early extubation, hand washing and sterile technique, limiting circuit changes, proper positioning with the head of the bed elevated, and supraglottic secretion drainage with mouth care. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 48, 49"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with no immune problems received the updated 2026-2027 coronavirus disease 2019 vaccine in September. He asks whether he needs anything more this season. Which plan is appropriate?","choices":["No further dose this season","Monthly doses through spring","A second dose about 6 months later","A second dose in 4 weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An updated 2026-2027 vaccine is recommended for adults 18 or older, and adults 65 or older generally need two seasonal doses about 6 months apart. At 72 he needs a second dose about 6 months after September. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with no medical conditions has community-acquired pneumonia and is stable for outpatient treatment. He had anaphylaxis to amoxicillin as a child, and local pneumococcal resistance to macrolides is 35%. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Azithromycin","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Doxycycline","Amoxicillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A healthy outpatient without resistant-organism risk takes amoxicillin or doxycycline. His amoxicillin anaphylaxis removes amoxicillin, and macrolide monotherapy needs local pneumococcal resistance below 25%, which 35% fails, so doxycycline is the remaining first-line choice. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man explored a cave with heavy bat droppings 2 weeks ago. He now has high fever, chills, myalgias, a nonproductive cough, chest pain and a headache. Which test should be ordered?","choices":["Chlamydia psittaci nucleic acid test","Urine Histoplasma antigen","Routine sputum bacterial culture","Serum cryptococcal antigen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Exposure to bat droppings followed by fever, chills, myalgias, a dry cough, chest pain and headache within 3 to 17 days fits acute histoplasmosis. Testing uses the urine Histoplasma antigen, often with serum antigen and antibody tests. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 75, 76, 77"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman vomits and aspirates during a witnessed seizure. Two hours later she has abrupt shortness of breath and hypoxemia, a temperature of 100.6°F and a white blood cell count of 13,500/µL. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Supportive care, no antibiotic","Ampicillin-sulbactam","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Antibiotic with anaerobic coverage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A witnessed aspiration of gastric contents followed within hours by abrupt respiratory decline is aspiration pneumonitis, a sterile chemical injury; fever or leukocytosis can occur without bacterial infection. Support oxygenation and the airway, and avoid prophylactic antibiotics when bacterial infection is not suspected. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 50, 51, 126"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man has acute bronchitis and is not given an antibiotic. He asks what to expect from his illness. Which counseling statement is accurate?","choices":["An antibiotic would halve the cough","Green sputum means it is bacterial","The cough often lasts 2 to 3 weeks","The cough should clear in 3 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute bronchitis is usually a self-limited viral infection, and the cough commonly lasts 2 to 3 weeks and may persist longer. Setting that expectation, and explaining that antibiotics do not significantly shorten the illness, is how unneeded antibiotic requests are reduced. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 17, 18, 21"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman who is 20 weeks pregnant and has diabetes tests positive for both influenza and coronavirus disease 2019 on a multiplex test. She has mild symptoms that began yesterday and a normal oxygen saturation. Which antiviral should be avoided because of her pregnancy?","choices":["Molnupiravir","Oseltamivir","Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir","Remdesivir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Molnupiravir is an adult alternative used only when the preferred coronavirus treatments are unsuitable, and it is avoided during pregnancy. Her pregnancy makes it the drug to avoid, while the influenza is treated with oseltamivir, which is preferred in pregnancy. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 104, 108, 120"}]};
